<pdd-reason>The test-churn gate: refuses to overwrite an existing test file when regeneration would rewrite more of it than the configured threshold, so accumulated regression coverage is not discarded.</pdd-reason>
<pdd-interface>
{
  "type": "module",
  "module": {
    "functions": [
      {"name": "_verify_test_churn", "signature": "(existing_code: Optional[str], generated_code: str, prompt_name: str, output_path: Optional[str], prompt_content: Optional[str], adopted_human: bool = False) -> None", "returns": "None"},
      {"name": "_compute_test_churn_ratio", "signature": "(pre_text: str, post_text: str) -> float", "returns": "float"},
      {"name": "_calculate_test_churn_ratio", "signature": "(before: str, after: str) -> float", "returns": "float"},
      {"name": "_get_test_churn_threshold", "signature": "() -> float", "returns": "float"},
      {"name": "_is_test_output_path", "signature": "(output_path: Optional[str]) -> bool", "returns": "bool"},
      {"name": "_is_python_generation", "signature": "(language: Optional[str], output_path: Optional[str]) -> bool", "returns": "bool"},
      {"name": "_prompt_allows_test_churn", "signature": "(prompt_content: Optional[str]) -> bool", "returns": "bool"},
      {"name": "_find_default_test_files", "signature": "(tests_dir: Optional[str], code_file_path: Optional[str]) -> List[str]", "returns": "List[str]"}
    ]
  }
}
</pdd-interface>
<pdd-dependency>conformance/gate_errors_python.prompt</pdd-dependency>
<pdd-dependency>conformance/directives_python.prompt</pdd-dependency>

% You are an expert Python engineer. Implement the test-churn gate in
% `pdd/conformance/test_churn.py`.

<include>context/python_preamble.prompt</include>

% Responsibility
  Measuring how much of an existing test file a regeneration would rewrite, and
  refusing the write when that exceeds the threshold. It exists because a model
  asked to add one test will cheerfully rewrite the whole file.

% Non-responsibilities
  Does not write files. Callers snapshot the existing content and hand both
  versions here.

% Vocabulary
  - **churn ratio**: rewritten proportion of the pre-existing test file, 0.0-1.0.
  - **opt-out**: a `BREAKING-CHANGE:` directive whose verb's direct object is
    `test` or `tests`.

% Contract rules
<contract_rules>
  R1. The gate MUST permit the write, without computing anything, when the
      existing content is missing or blank, when the output path is not a test
      path, when the prompt opts out, or when `PDD_SKIP_TEST_CHURN_GATE` or
      `PDD_SKIP_CONFORMANCE` is enabled. The first disables only this gate; the
      second disables all conformance gates.
  R2. Otherwise the gate MUST raise `TestChurnError` when the churn ratio is
      strictly greater than the threshold, carrying both values and the pre and
      post line counts.
  R3. Pure additive growth MUST NOT count as churn - when no lines were removed
      the ratio is 0.0. A first generation, where nothing existed before, is
      likewise exempt.
  R4. The ratio MUST be computed from a unified diff of the two texts as the
      larger of added and removed line counts, divided by the pre-existing line
      count, capped at 1.0. Diff header lines do not count as changes.
  R5. `PDD_TEST_CHURN_THRESHOLD` defaults to `0.40` and MUST accept either a
      decimal ratio or a percent string such as `40%`. An unparseable value MUST
      log a warning and fall back to the default - it MUST NOT yield 0.0, which
      would silently disable the gate. The result is clamped to 0.0-1.0.
  R6. `adopted_human` (#1903) MUST only annotate the raised error. It MUST NOT
      change the gate decision.
</contract_rules>

% Test-path recognition
  A path is a test artifact when any of the following holds. These are observable
  classifications, and the case rules matter:

  - a path segment is `tests`, `__tests__`, or the singular `__test__` (the
    jest/vitest/Next.js co-located directory, #1903);
  - the filename starts with `test_`;
  - case-INSENSITIVELY, the filename ends with `.test.` or `.spec.` plus one of
    `ts`, `tsx`, `js`, `jsx`, `mjs`, `cjs` - the ESM/CJS variants cover adopted
    co-located tests (#1903);
  - case-INSENSITIVELY, the filename ends with `_test` or `_spec` plus any
    extension in `_LANGUAGE_TEST_FILE_EXTS`, covering sibling-file conventions
    such as Go's `handler_test.go` and Ruby's `widget_spec.rb`;
  - case-SENSITIVELY, the filename ends with a PascalCase JVM/.NET/Swift suffix:
    `Test.java`, `Tests.java`, `TestCase.java`, `IT.java`, `Test.kt`, `Tests.kt`,
    `Spec.kt`, `Test.scala`, `Tests.scala`, `Spec.scala`, `Test.groovy`,
    `Tests.groovy`, `Spec.groovy`, `Tests.swift`, `Test.cs`, `Tests.cs`.
    Case sensitivity is REQUIRED here: lowercasing would misclassify `latest.kt`,
    `manifest.java`, `request.scala` and `latest.groovy` as tests.

% Opt-out grammar
  Scanning only anchored directives, a directive opts out when an opt-out verb -
  `rewrite`, `replace`, `regenerate`, `overwrite`, `churn`, `remove` or `drop`,
  in any inflection, case-insensitively - directly governs `test`/`tests`. A
  comma, semicolon, or one of the conjunctions `and`, `but`, `then`, `or`,
  `plus`, `also` between the verb and its object breaks the phrase, meaning the
  object belongs to a different verb. Required behavior:

  | Directive | Opts out |
  |---|---|
  | `BREAKING-CHANGE: rewriting the failing tests` | yes |
  | `BREAKING-CHANGE: rewrite the test suite for new helper` | yes |
  | `BREAKING-CHANGE: drop foo and rewrite tests` | yes - the second verb governs `tests` |
  | `BREAKING-CHANGE: rewrite docs and update tests` | no - `rewrite` governs `docs`, and `update` is not an opt-out verb |

% Inputs and outputs
  `_calculate_test_churn_ratio` is a backward-compatible alias of
  `_compute_test_churn_ratio`. `_is_python_generation` reports whether the target
  is Python by language name or `.py` extension. `_find_default_test_files`
  locates existing `test_<stem>.py` files for a module, returning an empty list
  when either input is missing or the directory does not exist.

% Dependencies
  Standard library, plus `TestChurnError` and `_LANGUAGE_TEST_FILE_EXTS` from
  `.gate_errors` and the directive helpers from `.directives`. The same gate is reused
  by `cmd_test_main` and one-session agentic sync; append and merge modes are
  exempt because they preserve the existing body.
